<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yorubaland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yorubaland is the cultural homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba people</a>, covering south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>. It held many kingdoms, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a>, regarded as the place of origin, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>, which dominated the region from the 17th to the early 19th century. After Oyo's collapse, wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibadan">Ibadan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilorin">Ilorin</a>, Ijebu and the Ekiti Parapo divided it until British rule in the 1890s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yorubaland.jpg" alt="Yorubaland" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oramfe · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yorubaland_Cultural_Area_of_West_Africa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Yorubaland covers about 142,114 square kilometres, of which 74.6 percent lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, 18.9 percent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> and 6.5 percent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>. It runs north from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a> and west from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a>, from near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lokoja">Lokoja</a> into Togo in the north and along the coast to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-novo">Porto-Novo</a>, beyond which Gbe speakers predominate. Mangrove forests, estuaries and coastal plains rise northward to the Yorubaland plateau, whose highest point is the Idanre Inselberg Hills at over 1,050 metres. The south is tropical high forest and the north and west woodland savanna, with the harmattan blowing from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> in the dry season.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tradition regards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a> as the progenitor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, and almost every Yoruba settlement traces its origin to princes of Ile-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a>, the cultural and spiritual homeland of the nation. Linguistic evidence suggests that the east of the region was settled earlier than the west. Ife had a golden age between 1100 and 1400 before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a> surpassed it. Sixteen established kingdoms are said to descend from Oduduwa. Most states were ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Obas</a>, priestly monarchs, with councils of chiefs called Oloyes; the balance of power varied, but a ruler who showed dictatorial tendencies could be sent an aroko, often parrot eggs in a covered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabash">calabash</a>, ordering him to give up the throne, usually by taking his own life.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> of Uthman Dan Fodio, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilorin">Ilorin</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-ile">Oyo-Ile</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a> capital; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibadan">Ibadan</a> and the Ijebu kingdom checked further advance. The other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> broke free of Oyo and fought among themselves, and in this period millions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> were forcibly transported to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and elsewhere. In 1892 the British went to war with the Ijebu Kingdom over its barriers to trade and occupied its capital, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> treaties then brought the rest of Yorubaland under British rule. Almost a fifth of it, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa</a> to Old Oyo and Kabba, was joined to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-region-nigeria">Northern Nigeria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In Nigeria Yorubaland comprises the states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a>, Osun, Ogun, Kwara, Ondo, Ekiti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> and the west of Kogi, and it extends into the Ouémé, Plateau and Collines departments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> and the Plateaux and Centrale regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>. It is home to an estimated 55 million people, most of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yorubaland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ibadan came into existence in 1829, in the turmoil that then characterised <b>Yorubaland</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ibadan">Ibadan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Oyo Empire began in the savannah woodlands of northern <b>Yorubaland</b>, north of the dense rainforest zone that made cavalry-based states difficult to form across much of coastal West Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 1850s Ibadan dominated the Ekiti and Ijesha towns of eastern <b>Yorubaland</b>, collecting tribute through resident agents.</blockquote><a href="/state/ekiti-parapo">Ekiti Parapo</a></li><li><blockquote>Ilorin's cavalry raided deep into <b>Yorubaland</b> and helped destroy the old Oyo capital in the 1830s, but Ibadan defeated it at Osogbo around 1840, halting the jihad's advance south.</blockquote><a href="/state/ilorin-emirate">Ilorin Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
